3984,1970-137,1970-137-01,323,1970,COOLIDGE v. NEW HAMPSHIRE,1.0,1.0,"In the wake of a ""particularly brutal"" murder of a fourteen-year-old girl, the New Hampshire Attorney General took charge of police activities relating to the murder. When the police applied for a warrant to search suspect Edward Coolidge's automobile, the Attorney General, acting as a justice of the peace, authorized it. Additionally, local police had taken items from Coolidge's home during the course of an interview with the suspect's wife. Coolidge was found guilty and sentenced to life imprisonment.
