4135,1971-126,1971-126-01,70-5061,1971,KIRBY v. ILLINOIS,1.0,0.0,"William Shard reported to the Chicago police that two men stole his wallet.  The wallet contained traveler’s checks and his social security card, among other things.  The next day, two police officers stopped Thomas Kirby and his friend, Ralph Bean.  When asked for identification, Kirby produced Shard’s wallet.  The officers arrested Kirby and Bean and brought them to the Maxwell Street Police Station.  Once there, the officers learned about Shard’s robbery and sent a car to pick up Shard and bring him to the station.  Without an attorney present, police asked Shard if Kirby and Bean were his robbers.  Shard instantly gave a positive identification.   Kirby and Bean were not indicted until almost 6 weeks later.  At trial, Kirby unsuccessfully attempted to suppress Shard’s identification.  The jury found Kirby guilty and the Appellate Court of Illinois, First District affirmed.
