6634,1984-100,1984-100-01,83-1632,1984,"HARPER & ROW, PUBLISHERS, INC., et al. v. NATION ENTERPRISES et al.",8.0,1.0,"In 1977, former President Gerald Ford contracted with Harper &amp; Row, Publishers, Inc. to publish his memoirs. Harper &amp; Row negotiated a prepublication agreement with Time Magazine for the right to excerpt 7,500 words from Ford's account of his pardon of former President Richard Nixon. Before Time released its article, an unauthorized source provided The Nation Magazine with the unpublished Ford manuscript. Subsequently, The Nation, using approximately 300 words from the manuscript, scooped Time. Harper &amp; Row sued The Nation, alleging violations of the Copyright Revision Act of 1976. The District Court held that The Nation's use of the copyrighted material constituted infringement. In reversing, the Court of Appeals held that Nation's use of the copyrighted material was sanctioned as a fair use.
