7839,1991-101,1991-101-01,91-594,1991,AMERICAN NATIONAL RED CROSS v. S. G. AND A. E.,9.0,1.0,"Plaintiffs filed two state-law tort actions in New Hampshire state courts, alleging that one of them had contracted AIDS from a transfusion of contaminated blood during surgery. The second action was brought against the Red Cross after plaintiffs discovered that it had supplied the tainted blood. Before the state court could decide a motion to consolidate the cases, the Red Cross invoked the federal removal statute, 28 U.S.C. Section 1441, to remove the second suit to federal court. The district court rejected the plaintiffs' motion to remand the case to state court, holding that the Red Cross' charter provision allowing it to ""sue and be sued in courts of law and equity, State or Federal, within the jurisdiction of the United States,"" 36 U.S.C. Section 2, conferred original jurisdiction on the federal district court.
